<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="fatty acid biosynthesis initiation (type II)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4381" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Fatty acids are key building blocks for the phospholipid components of cell membranes and are determinants of intracellular communication, in the form of lipid second messengers |CITS: [11094259]|, and fatty acyl moieties of proteins that modify their location and function |CITS: [10446384]|. There are two basic types of fatty acid (FAS) biosynthesis mechanisms. The type I system is found in mammals and lower eukaryotes. The mammalian system consists of a single gene product that contains all of the reaction centers required to produce a fatty acid (see |FRAME: CPLX-7628| from |FRAME: TAX-9606|), while the system of lower eukaryotes (such as yeast) consists of two genes, whose polypeptide products combine to form a multifunctional complex (see |FRAME: CPLX-7627| from |FRAME: TAX-4932|). Type II systems are found in bacteria, plants |CITS: [15952903]|, parasites of the |FRAME: TAX-5794| phylum |CITS: [17112527]| and mitochondria |CITS: [12882974][12654921]|. The reactions in these systems are catalyzed by a series of individual soluble proteins that are each encoded by a discrete gene, and the pathway intermediates are transferred between the enzymes as thioesters of |FRAME: ACP|. The best studied pathway is that of |FRAME: TAX-83333|, from which all the enzymes have been purified and crystalized. In plants fatty acid synthesis occurs mainly in plastids of leaf mesophyll cells, seeds, and oil-accumulating fruits. Although most of the synthesis occurrs in plastids, it has been shown that mitochondria are also capable of synthesizing fatty acids |CITS: [14660674]|. Fatty acid biosynthesis starts with an initiation sequence that produces |FRAME: Acetoacetyl-ACPs|, an activated molecule that is used for subsequent elongation reactions, which ultimately produce the final fatty acid products. This pathway describes only the initiation sequence, subsequent elongation reactions are described in the pathways |FRAME: FASYN-ELONG-PWY|, |FRAME: PWY-5971| and |FRAME: PWY0-862|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: TAX-83333| has several different routes in which it can produce acetoacetyl-ACP, and this pathway describes the major route. In it, one |FRAME: ACETYL-COA| molecule is converted to |FRAME: MALONYL-COA|, which is then activated to |FRAME: MALONYL-ACP| that is condensed with a second |FRAME: ACETYL-COA| molecule to form |FRAME: Acetoacetyl-ACPs|. The first enzyme in this pathway, |FRAME: ACETYL-COA-CARBOXYLMULTI-CPLX|, is the controlling point for the flux of carbon into lipids. It is a biotin-containing multi-enzyme complex with two activities, |FRAME: BIOTIN-CARBOXYL-CPLX| and |FRAME: ACETYL-COA-CARBOXYLTRANSFER-CPLX|, catalyzing two half reactions that combined condense |FRAME: HCO3| with |FRAME: ACETYL-COA| to yield |FRAME: MALONYL-COA|. Further discussion of this enzyme is available in the pathway |FRAME: PWY0-1264|. In plants this reaction is catalyzed by the plastidic heteromeric |FRAME: CPLX-6282|. A second, homomeric form of the enzyme is cytosolic and is involved in very-long-chain fatty acid biosynthesis and synthesis of flavonoids. The |FRAME: MALONYL-COA| is next attached to |FRAME: ACP| by the enzyme |FRAME: MALONYL-COA-ACP-TRANSACYL-MONOMER|. The last step of the pathway, the condensation of a second |FRAME: ACETYL-COA| with |FRAME: MALONYL-ACP malonyl-[acp]|, is carried out by the condensing enzyme &amp;beta;-ketoacyl-ACP synthase (KAS). |FRAME: TAX-83333|, as well as most plants, has three &amp;beta;-ketoacyl-ACP synthases: |FRAME: FABB-CPLX|, |FRAME: 3-OXOACYL-ACP-SYNTHII-CPLX| and |FRAME: CPLX0-252|, encoded by |FRAME: EG10274|, |FRAME: EG12606| and |FRAME: EG10277|, respectively. |FRAME: CPLX0-252| is the key enzyme in the initiation of fatty acids biosynthesis. It selectively catalyzes the formation of acetoacetyl-ACP and specifically uses CoA thioesters rather than acyl-ACP as the primer. The products tend to be shorter than the products of KASI and II, which are involved primarily in elongation reactions. Unlike the other two enzymes, KASIII cannot participate in the terminal elongation steps of fatty acid biosynthesis |CITS: [15952903]|. It should be noted that in some bacteria, including |FRAME: TAX-208964|, a different enzyme, encoded by the |FRAME: G-15300| gene, catalyzes the condensation of |FRAME: ACETYL-COA| with |FRAME: MALONYL-ACP malonyl-[acp]| |CITS: [22753059]|. KASIII is inhibited by long-chain acyl-ACPs, indicating a role in feedback regulation of fatty acid synthesis. Since it catalyzes the first condensation step in fatty acid synthesis, it is able to control the rate of fatty acid initiation. The substrate specificity of |FRAME: CPLX0-252| is a major determining factor in membrane fatty acid composition of an organism. In |FRAME: TAX-562| the enzyme is highly selective for acetyl-CoA, and thus only straight-chain fatty acids are produced in this organism. In many gram-positive bacteria, such as |FRAME: TAX-1423|, the enzyme prefers branched-chain acyl-CoA substrates derived from amino acid metabolism, leading to the production of primarily iso- and anteisobranched fatty acids |CITS:[10629181]|. Other potential routes for fatty acid biosynthesis initiation in |FRAME: TAX-83333| are described in |FRAME: PWY-5966| and |FRAME: PWY-5965|.</Description>
